Installation

The following sections describe the MS-DOS* and UNIX** installation procedure
for the DSP1610 Support Software Tools.
The following procedures assume that the SGS tools (version 3.2.1 or later)
have already been installed (see the Release 3.2.1 DSP1600 Family SGS
release notes and sgsread.1st file for further details)


MS-DOS Installation

The MS-DOS distribution comes on two 3.5 inch floppy disks.  The software runs
on 386 or higher PC with MS-DOS 4.0 or later and at least 2 megabytes of
extended memory.

The installation of the DSP1610 tools includes two steps.  First, the
installation routine copies the necessary software from the distribution
diskettes to your hard drive.  Second, the installation routine creates a
file named NEW-VARS.BAT containing the environment variables (IFILE and
LIB1600).  If the user allows the default, the installation automatically
adds these variables to the AUTOEXEC.BAT file.  To perform the installation,
follow the steps below.

        1)  Place the disk marked "DSP1610-ST-MSDOS" into the floppy drive
            of the host PC.
        2)  Enter "a:install" at the MS-DOS prompt (this assumes the diskette
            is in drive a:).
        3)  Answer the questions presented by the install routine.

Before copying the files to the hard drive, the install routine prompts the
user for the distribution diskette drive, the boot drive, and the installation
directory. If the installation directory contains a previous release of the
tools, the install routine will issue a warning and allow the user to quit the
process.  The user may then start the install procedure again, specifying a
new directory.  If the installation is proceeding correctly, the install
routine will prompt the user for additional diskettes.  For more details on
the install procedure, please consult the DSP1610 Support Tools Manual For
Release 3.4.

It is VERY IMPORTANT that the PATH variable be properly setup as described
in NEW-VARS.BAT.  If an older version of cmd1610.exe is found first in the
PATH, the win1610.exe tool will not be able to locate the DOS extender
embedded in cmd1610.exe (see Release 3.4.2 Features below).

UNIX Installation

The UNIX distribution comes on 1/4 inch data cartridge or one 3.5 inch
floppy disk.  The software runs on a Sparc*** processor running SunOS 4.1.3.

The UNIX installation of the DSP1610 tools includes two steps.  First, the
user copies the necessary software from the distribution medium to the
hard drive.  Second, the user must modify the .profile or .login files to
include the environment variables.  To perform the installation, change to
the directory where the tools are to be installed and follow the steps below.

        1) copy the files:
           If the distribution media is a 3.5" floppy:

                - Place the diskette in the floppy drive of the host system.
                - Enter "bar xvZf /dev/fd0a" at the prompt.

           If the distribution media is a 1/4" data cartridge:

                - Place the cartridge into the tape drive of the host system.
                - Enter "tar xvf /dev/rst0" at the prompt.

        2) Edit your .profile or .login and add the following lines
           (m2_12ka.if can be any suitable ifile):

           If you run the sh(1) or ksh(1) shell, the lines are
                LIB1600=<the tools directory>
                IFILE=$LIB1600/m2_12ka.if
                PATH=$PATH:$LIB1600
                export LIB1600 IFILE PATH

           If you run the csh (1), the lines are
                setenv LIB1600 <the tools directory>
                setenv IFILE $LIB1600/m2_12ka.if
                setenv path=($path $LIB1600)


Packing List

The following section presents the packing list for both the MS-DOS and UNIX
distributions.


MS-DOS Packing List

The following is the MS-DOS packing list for the 3.4.2 release of the
DSP1610 Support Software Tools:

cmd1610.exe	- the DSP1610 command-based simulator
win1610.exe	- the DSP1610 window-based simulator
bphelp.txt	- interactive help on the breakpoint menu
cmd.hlp		- a help data file used by cmd1610 and win1610
dhelp.exe	- a stand alone help facility
menu1610.rsc	- the menu file for win1610
read1610.1st	- a text file with useful information
install.com     - an installation utility which does not get copied to the PC
                  Hard Drive
toollist.txt    - the tools list used by the install routine. This file does
                  not get copied to the PC hard drive.
1610.h		- Chip specific header file used by as1600
hw_test.exe	- a utility used to test the installation of the
		  DSP1600 HDS hardware
cfig386.exe    - a DOS extender configuration utility
pfix1610.bat   - a batch file to assist with cfig386.exe
pfxagent.bat   - a batch file used by pfix1610.bat
pfxmklst.bat   - a batch file used by pfix1610.bat

UNIX Packing List

The following is the UNIX packing list for the 3.4.2 release of the
DSP1610 Support Software Tools:

cmd1610         - the DSP1610 command-based simulator
cmd.hlp		- a help data file used by cmd1610 and win1610
dhelp		- a stand alone help facility
read1610.1st    - a text file with useful information
1610.h		- Chip specific header file used by as1600


Description

The DSP1610-ST 3.4.2 release fixes many previously reported exceptions and
adds several enhancements to the last production release (2.1.4) of the
DSP1610 tools.  The following sections review these changes.


Release 3.4.2 Limitations/Warnings

1.HDS trace buffer - When using software breakpoints, the HDS trace buffer
  may not reflect reality.  If a software breakpoint is encountered, the trace
  buffer will contain two instances of the instruction that caused the
  breakpoint.

2.When the memory map is changed under program control (by setting the alf
  register), win1610 immediately displays the code pointed to by the new map,
  even though the next instruction is (correctly) executed from the old map.
  This can be confusing if a user single-steps through a memory-map change.

3.Numerous improvements to the simulator chip models have been incorporated.
  Cache operations, SIO, vector processing, and reset processing, are all
  modeled in exactly the way the chip behaves.  These changes may have effects
  on the behavior of the simulator (see "Release 3.4 differences/fixes" below.)


Release 3.4.2 Features

1.New IFILE Names - The following ifiles should be used for the DSP1610:

       m1_12ka.if - Memory Map 1
       m2_12ka.if - Memory Map 2
       m3_12ka.if - Memory Map 3
       m4_12ka.if - Memory Map 4

The remainder of this file describes changes common to all 3.4 simulators.
cmd16xx/win16xx in the case of the DSP1610-ST means cmd1610/win1610.

The following features are supported by all release 3.4 cmd16xx and win16xx
tools in both simulator and HDS modes.

2.Support for extended DOS memory - All of the tools in this package now
  make use of the available extended memory in the user's PC.  The memory
  limitations which the earlier versions of these tools imposed have been
  lifted.  However, even though the new tools use extended memory, it is
  recommended that at least 400K of DOS memory be available. The code that
  allows execution in the extended DOS environment (DOS extender) is
  cmd16xx.exe.  All executable tools in this release use the embedded DOS
  extender code in cmd16xx.exe.  For execution of the support tools, the user
  needs to have the location of cmd16xx.exe in the PATH.  If the required
  tool with the embedded extender code is not in the PATH, an error message
  similar to "Stub ldr err 7: DOS-Extender file cmd16xx.exe not found" is
  issued.  Also, if an older version of the support tools is found in the
  PATH before cmd16xx.exe from this package, unpredictable behavior will
  occur.

3.The "assem" command is now usable on DOS.  The user should have at least
  400K of conventional DOS memory available or "assem" will fail.

4.A new simulator variable 'acycles' counts cycles only when the processor
  is awake.  In every other way, 'acycles' and 'cycles' are similar.  The
  processor may be put to sleep, for example, using the AWAIT bit in the
  alf register.

5.Previous versions of cmd16xx/win16xx looked for a startup command script
  file called "cmds" in the current working directory and the $HOME
  directory or, if $HOME isn't set, the root directory.  The simulator
  now searches the $LIB1600 directory also.


Release 3.4 Differences/Fixes

The following changes apply to all release 3.4 cmd16xx and win16xx tools.
The changes can cause behavior differences between release 3.4 simulator/HDS
output and previous tool releases.

1.Simulator interface modifications (simulator and HDS modes)
  display "R" now prints only registers (not stime, ibfint, etc.)

  display "I" now prints all internal variables (including stime, ibfint, etc.)

  Users may now issue the ".V" command at the 'cmd>' prompt to display
    simulator version information.
 
2.Simulator model modifications (simulator only)
  Modified mwait register initialization.  The interaction of EXM and INT1
    during reset is now modeled correctly.  The mwait register is initialized
    to 0xffff when EXM is 1 and INT1 is 0.  This means that users who wish to
    model fast ERAM/EROM must either place a "mwait = 0x0000" into their code
    that handles reset, or set the INT1 pin to 1 ("int1 = 1") in the simulator
    before typing reset and then set the INT1 pin to 0 before typing cont.
    For example, to provide backward compatible reset and run capability use:
	cmd> reset_nw = { int1 = 1; reset; int1 = 0 }
	cmd> run_nw = { int1 = 1; reset; int1 = 0; cont }

  Better modeling of I/O and trap processing.  Improved modeling of I/O and
    interrupt processing may cause timing changes in simulated tests.
    Breakpoint and error messages may now occur at different simulation
    times (stime).

  SIO mode simulation now properly implements bit reversal in both pin mode
    and file mode I/O.  For more details see section 5.4.6 of the
    "DSP16xx Support Tool Manual For Release 3.4" 

  Deterministic arithmetic operations using undefined values now give correct
    results.  For example, if a0 is undefined and y and yl are 0:

	a1 = a0 ^ a0		/* a1 result is 0 */
	a1 = a0 - a0		/* a1 result is 0 */
	a1 = a0 & y		/* a1 result is 0 */

    Similarly, if a0 is undefined and y and yl are 0xffff:

	a1 = a0 | y		/* a1 result is 0xffffffff */

  If an operation places the chip model in a sleep mode (STOP pin, AWAIT bit,
    etc.), statistics, trace information, and number of instructions executed
    are now correctly displayed.

  The 'stat' command now handles wait state situations correctly.

  The F1 Y operation (multiply/ALU operation with post-modification of pointer
    register) does not actually access the Y space location since no
    destination is specified for the Y access.  The simulator no longer
    issues the warning:

	"internal RAM access out of range"

    when the address contained in the Y side pointer register point outside of
    allocated memory.

  The pipe-lined instruction execution of the DSP1600 family causes
    unexpected behavior when the direct branch (goto JA) instruction
    is located in either of the last 2 locations of a 4K page.  The
    simulator will now correctly simulate this situation and also issue
    a warning.  For further information, see the Note for 'goto JA' and
    "call JA" in the Control Instructions section of chapter 5.0 (Software
    Architecture) in the DSP16xx data sheet.

  Previously the command "BPSET SW label" would not set a breakpoint in
    simulator mode.  An error message indicating that SW breakpoints are only
    used in HDS mode was displayed even though the command "BPSET HW label"
    operated correctly in simulator mode.  Now, to allow a single script file
    for both simulation and emulation, if all other conditions favor setting
    the breakpoint, a warning is issued telling the user that an xmem bkpt will
    be set instead.  The type is then changed to xmem, and the bkpt is set.

  Output from the logsigs command now uses the following conventions for
    bit-wide signals (pins).  Inputs and I/O pins operating as inputs:
	0 - logic low
	1 - logic high

    Outputs and I/O pins operating as outputs:
	L - logic low
	H - logic high

  The variables ibfint, obeint, pidsint, podsint, ibf2int, and obe2int
    control IO interrupt generation for file mode IO (the default).
    These variables are (and always have been) initialized to 32 cycles.
    If, at reset, mwait is initialized with all 1's, after only a few
    instructions all of these conditions are asserted in the ins register.
    Generation of the ins status indication can be disabled by setting the
    corresponding variable(s) to 0.  All interrupts are masked by default
    in the inc register after reset.

3.win16xx's source window can be scrolled up & down via the scroll arrows
  on the windows right border.  The source code will scroll by either 1
  line at a time or a half a window at a time, depending on whether the
  customer is using the left or right mouse buttons, respectively.

4.HDS modifications (HDS only)
  a) "display dsp" command has new state: "Trapped in Cache"
  b) "bpcount" now properly resets to zero when the device reaches its
      final breakpoint (this matches the simulator mode of operation).
      Note: If it is manually stopped with a ^C, "bpcount" retains its
      previous value.



cfig386

The DOS tools releases now include a utility to configure the DOS extender
stubs included in the executables.  This may be necessary when the tools are
installed in a directory that is not in the PATH, or when another set of DSP
tools is installed in a directory that is before them in the PATH.  Its usage
is as follows:

cfig386 -dosxname file_with_extender file_without_stub

The assembler and command-line simulator files contain a special DOS extender
that allows them to access memory above the normal limits of DOS.  The other
tools each contain a small stub that loads the DOS extender from one of these
files.  When the tools are first installed, the stubs are configured to access
the assembler or command-line simulator by searching the DOS PATH.  Normally,
this does not cause any problems.  However, if an earlier version of the
assembler or simulator is in the PATH before the intended one, this load will
fail.

If this problem occurs, it may be corrected by using cfig386 to encode the full
path name of the assembler or command-line simulator into the stub files.
Batch files have been provided with each of the tools to automate this process.
The file pfix1600.bat corrects the SGS files, and pfixXXXX.bat (where XXXX is
the chip name) corrects the simulator files.

None of these utilities should be used unless problems are experienced with the
tools.  Once the full path name of the executables is encoded into the stubs,
the tools cannot be moved to a different directory without changing the path
name using these utilities.


